(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-medical-literature-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Medical Literature Master Exam — Form E","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is a prospective cohort study?","choices":["Groups are assembled now by exposure and followed forward","The investigator goes back in history to define a risk group","Exposure is assigned at random by the investigator","Groups are defined by outcome and examined for past exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with baseline data collected at the outset. It is also called a concurrent cohort study, because observation runs alongside the events. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which designs are given as examples of descriptive work?","choices":["Cohort and case-control studies","Case report and case series","Systematic reviews and meta-analyses","Randomized controlled trials"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and both sit low in the evidence hierarchy precisely because describing a group supports no comparison and therefore no inference about cause. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What principle underlies years of potential life lost?","choices":["Death at a younger age loses more productive years","All deaths carry equal weight regardless of age","Deaths should be weighted by the cost of treatment","Only deaths from preventable causes are counted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the practical use follows: it helps target interventions at the causes that take the most life from a population, not merely the most lives. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What kind of data does the National Center for Health Statistics gather?","choices":["Periodic surveys of disease and disability","Financial data on health care spending by household","Continuous registration of every birth and death","Disease-specific data collected by state governments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its examples include the National Health Interview Survey and the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, both periodic rather than continuous registrations. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What proportion of values falls within one standard deviation of the mean in a normal distribution?","choices":["About half","About 95 percent","Essentially all of them","About two thirds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and 95 percent fall within two standard deviations, which is where the conventional reference range comes from. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is another name for an extraneous variable?","choices":["A dependent variable","A sample","An independent variable","A covariate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the definition is functional: it is a variable that may affect the relationship between the independent and dependent variables, which is what makes it worth identifying. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is an advantage of median survival time?","choices":["It requires no follow-up of patients","It adjusts for the age of the patients","It uses every death in the cohort","It can be found before all have died"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it is also less affected by extreme values, so a few very long or short survivors do not move it much. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does a frequency distribution portray?","choices":["The average value across the sample","The proportion of people who develop a condition","The spread of values around the mean","The quantity in each category, class or interval"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, showing the number or proportion of people with different values. It can be used on nominal variables as well as interval data. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"When is an inception cohort assembled?","choices":["At the time of the outcome event","When the disease first becomes apparent","Once symptoms have peaked in severity","After treatment has been completed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the aim is to assemble it as early as possible and as uniformly as possible. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Between what values must a probability lie?","choices":["Zero and one hundred","One and ten","Zero and one","Minus one and one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with zero meaning the event cannot occur and one meaning it certainly will. All the probabilities of events in a sample must sum to one. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the case-fatality rate?","choices":["Deaths from a disease per 100,000 population","Percent of patients whose disease is undetectable","Percent of patients who improve after treatment","Percent of patients with a disease who die of it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and because its denominator is patients with the disease it measures the severity of the disease itself. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is chance in medical research?","choices":["Random variation producing conclusions that differ from the true results","The probability that a patient will develop a given disease","Systematic error producing conclusions that differ from the true results","A deliberate decision to report only favorable findings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the crucial property is that it is random: it is equally likely to produce a result higher or lower than the truth, unlike bias, which pushes one way. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What may inter-patient variation be due to?","choices":["Only errors made by the observer","Only inherent biological differences","Only differences in how the measurement was taken","Biological differences, measurement conditions, and measurement error"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it matters because answers to clinical questions about an individual are most often derived from groups of patients with similar conditions. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which study designs allow surveillance to support hypothesis testing for etiology?","choices":["Case reports and case series","Randomized controlled trials only","Case-control or cohort studies","Cross-sectional surveys only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and this is where surveillance hands over to analytic epidemiology: the data identify a question that a comparative design is then built to answer. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How is the rule-of-thumb probability shift applied?","choices":["It is added to the pre-test probability","It is subtracted from the specificity","It replaces the pre-test probability","It is multiplied by the pre-test probability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why the same likelihood ratio produces a very different answer depending on how likely the disease was before the test. — Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios"},{"q":"What do the letters A, B and C in a recommendation grade indicate?","choices":["The quality of the supporting evidence","The size of the patient group","The cost of the treatment","The strength of the recommendation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the letters express confidence in the estimate of effect, with A the highest, based on the type and quality of studies behind it. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why should a reader ask whether study patients are similar to their own?","choices":["To judge whether the results apply","To check that outcomes were assessed objectively","To confirm that randomization worked","To decide whether the sample size was adequate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it is a check on applying the study to the patient in front of you, the same concern as generalizability. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What defines a random sample?","choices":["Researchers take whoever they can find","Every individual has an equal chance of selection","Participants are whoever is convenient to obtain","Every individual has a known chance of selection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is what separates it from a probability sample, where the chance of selection is known but need not be equal. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the practice of evidence-based medicine integrate?","choices":["Published guidelines with local hospital policy","Clinical expertise with external evidence from systematic research","External evidence with institutional cost constraints","Clinical expertise with the opinion of senior colleagues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and clinical expertise is defined concretely as the proficiency and judgment gleaned from clinical practice, so it is experience disciplined by evidence rather than either alone. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What protects against performance bias?","choices":["Blinding of study participants","Disclosing conflicts of interest","Increasing the sample size","Publishing the study protocol in advance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the mechanism follows directly from the example: a participant who does not know they are on placebo has no reason to go looking for other treatment. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which variables are considered first for inclusion in a model?","choices":["Those least associated with the outcome","Those with the most missing data","Those easiest to measure in every patient","Those most strongly associated with the outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: after identifying variables related to the outcome, including confounders and effect modifiers, those with the strongest association are considered for inclusion. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which study design typically evaluates immunizations?","choices":["Randomized controlled trial","Case-control study","Cross-sectional survey","Case series"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunization studies are typically randomized controlled trials, as are counseling approaches. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A test with sensitivity 95 percent and specificity 98 percent screens 20,000 people, of whom 0.5 percent have the disease. What is the positive predictive value?","choices":["About 98 percent","About 19 percent","About 81 percent","About 95 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There are 95 true positives (95 of 100) and 398 false positives (2 percent of 19,900), so 95 divided by 493 is about 19 percent. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is effect modification?","choices":["Results vary randomly between samples","A variable changes the effect of exposure","Participants drop out during follow-up","A third factor distorts the association"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the presence or absence of the variable changes the effect of the exposure, and the change may be synergistic or antagonistic. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is described as eminence-based medicine?","choices":["Substituting volume and stridency for evidence","Senior colleagues holding experience to trump evidence","Fear of litigation driving over-investigation","Sartorial elegance and verbal eloquence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list of alternatives to evidence that each substitute something else for appraisal: volume, eloquence, timidity, fear of litigation or bravado. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are prevalence studies good and bad at?","choices":["Good for following disease over many years","Good for hypotheses and service planning, poor for cause and effect","Good for establishing cause, poor for planning services","Good for measuring the severity of a condition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they are relatively inexpensive, which is part of why they are a sensible first look at what is common in a community. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is needed to detect a difference between exposed and unexposed groups when the risk is small?","choices":["Fewer unexposed persons","A larger affected population","A shorter follow-up period","A single exposed patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a small risk produces few extra cases, so many more people must be affected before exposed and unexposed groups can be told apart. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A meta-analysis combines one high-quality trial with several seriously flawed studies. Which appraisal question does this raise?","choices":["Are the studies all of equal quality?","Were the excluded studies all listed?","Was a forest plot drawn for the review?","Did the reviewers write a protocol first?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the quality of the included studies should be examined, and mixing very different levels of quality weakens the pooled result. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the essential difference between a cohort and a case-control study?","choices":["Cohort is experimental, case-control is observational","Cohort is secondary research, case-control is primary","Cohort measures at one moment, case-control over time","Cohort groups by exposure, case-control groups by outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the direction of enquiry follows: the cohort looks forward from exposure to outcome, the case-control backward from outcome to exposure. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What question does descriptive evidence answer?","choices":["Which treatment is most effective for the diagnosis","Which test most accurately predicts the diagnosis","What characterizes patients with a specific diagnosis","Whether the diagnosis can be prevented"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It describes who has the condition rather than evaluating how it is detected or what should be done about it, which are the other two kinds of evidence named. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is true of a risk factor's effect on prognosis?","choices":["It always worsens prognosis","It sets the case-fatality rate","It need not worsen prognosis","It always improves prognosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: risk factors do not necessarily represent a poorer prognosis, so being at risk of a disease and faring badly with it are separate questions. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What should treatment decisions rest on, rather than coincidence, biased comparisons and wishful thinking?","choices":["Evidence on how treatments relate to outcomes","Agreement among clinicians who use the treatment","The treatment most commonly used in practice","Impressions from memorable individual patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because coincidence, biased comparisons and wishful thinking all mislead; evidence on the relationship between a treatment and its outcomes is the sound basis. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A guideline panel includes methodologic experts, clinicians and members of the affected population. Which standard is met?","choices":["Group composition","Updating","External review","Transparency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the panel is multidisciplinary and balanced, with a variety of methodologic experts, clinicians and populations affected by the guideline. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is said about cutoff points for normal and abnormal?","choices":["They are derived from measured healthy populations","They mark the point where disease begins","They are fixed by national agencies","They are often arbitrary"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the warning attached follows from it: be careful of labeling, since a label applied on an arbitrary threshold follows the patient afterwards. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A healthy adult receives an influenza vaccine. Which level of prevention is this?","choices":["Chemoprevention","Tertiary","Primary","Secondary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because vaccination removes the risk of infection before any disease exists, which is the aim of primary prevention. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Two trials enroll 250 and 750 participants. If a meta-analysis weights each in proportion to size, what share of the weight goes to the smaller trial?","choices":["50%","33%","25%","75%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: 250 of the 1,000 total participants is 25 percent, so the larger trial contributes three times as much weight as the smaller one. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When does high sensitivity matter most?","choices":["When the disease is highly prevalent","When a false positive would trigger risky further testing","When missing the disease would have serious consequences","When the test is expensive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because few false negatives is exactly the property needed when the cost of overlooking a case is high. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why should a screening test have high specificity?","choices":["To make the test cheaper","To reduce false negatives","To reduce false positives","To detect disease earlier"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a screened population most people are healthy, so specificity is what keeps healthy people from being wrongly flagged. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"In a cohort, 10 of 500 vaccinated and 40 of 500 unvaccinated persons developed influenza. What is the relative risk of influenza with vaccination?","choices":["0.06","4.0","0.02","0.25"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 0.02 divided by 0.08 is 0.25; a value below 1 means the vaccinated have lower risk, here one quarter of the risk in the unvaccinated. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which of these is named as an evidence-based medicine database?","choices":["The Florida Cancer Data System","TripPro","The Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance Survey","The National Health Interview Survey"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside Clinical Key and Essential Evidence Plus, which incorporates InfoPOEMS and InfoRetriever. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Two exposures each have a relative risk of 2.0. Baseline incidence is 1 in 1,000 for the first and 1 in 10 for the second. Which has the greater attributable risk?","choices":["Neither can be calculated","The first, with the lower baseline","The second, with the higher baseline","Both, because the relative risks are equal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: attributable risk is the difference in incidence, 0.1 for the second exposure (0.2 minus 0.1) against 0.001 for the first, so the same relative risk hides a huge gap in excess cases. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"In a trial of a new asthma inhaler, which is an exclusion criterion?","choices":["Frequent symptoms despite treatment","Allergy to a component of the inhaler","Willingness to be randomized","A confirmed diagnosis of asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a contraindication to a treatment under evaluation is an exclusion criterion, a factor that prevents inclusion. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why might a new treatment be tested for non-inferiority?","choices":["It may be cheaper, safer or easier","It avoids the need for randomization","It has no established comparison treatment","It is expected to be far more effective"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: if it is unlikely to be less effective but has such advantages, it can be preferable to the established treatment. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Researchers enroll 150 patients with a rare cancer and 300 without it, and ask about past exposure. Which measure of association suits this study?","choices":["Absolute risk","Attributable risk","Relative risk","Odds ratio"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the odds ratio is the measure of association for case-control studies, while relative risk is used for cohort studies. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How is causality defined?","choices":["Any factor that occurs before an outcome","Any factor associated with an outcome","A factor that helps produce the outcome","Any factor with a significant p value"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: causality exists if a factor produces or contributes to the production of a specified outcome, which allows for multiple causes. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does a measurement with low validity and high reliability look like?","choices":["Tightly clustered results on the true value","Widely scattered results centered on the true value","Widely scattered results away from the true value","Tightly clustered results, all away from the true value"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the most dangerous combination, because the consistency is easily mistaken for correctness when nothing is checked against a known standard. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What does it mean for information to be unfiltered?","choices":["The studies have not yet been peer reviewed","Nobody has appraised the primary studies for the reader","The studies have been excluded from systematic reviews","The findings have not been published"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so the appraisal falls to whoever is reading. Randomized trials, cohort and case-control studies all sit in that tier despite being sound designs. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What example is given of serial testing?","choices":["Rapid antigen testing alongside throat culture","Biopsy repeated after an interval","Endoscopy followed by a hypothetical upper gastrointestinal test","Exercise tolerance testing, then catheterization if positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, for coronary artery disease: the simpler, safer test runs first and the invasive one follows only on a positive result. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A trial of 40,000 patients finds a drug lowers systolic blood pressure by 1 millimeter of mercury, with a p value below 0.0001. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Clinically important but not statistically significant","Neither statistically nor clinically significant","Statistically significant but of doubtful clinical importance","Both statistically and clinically important"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: small differences can be statistically significant, especially in very large samples, yet a fall this small is unlikely to matter to patients. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is qualitative research characterized?","choices":["Experimental, with the investigator assigning the intervention","Naturalistic and inductive, developing descriptions","Secondary, analyzing data others have collected","Positivistic and deductive, developing predictions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its data are observation, field notes and audio recordings, drawn from both researchers and participants rather than from measurement. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Two treatments prevent the same outcome over the same period. Treatment X has a number needed to treat of 25 and Treatment Y has 60. Which conclusion follows?","choices":["Treatment X is more effective","They are equally effective","Treatment Y is more effective","Treatment X is more harmful"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A smaller number needed to treat means fewer patients must be treated for one to benefit, so X does more per patient. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What characterizes the preclinical phase of a disease?","choices":["Symptoms are present but no diagnosis exists","Treatment has begun but symptoms remain","Disease is present but symptoms have not appeared","The outcome has already occurred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and this is the window in which screening can find disease, before signs and symptoms bring the patient to care. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which bias arises from difficulty remembering past exposures?","choices":["Selection bias","Publication bias","Recall bias","Measurement bias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: participants' difficulty in remembering distorts the reported exposure or history. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What should a systematic review list for each excluded study?","choices":["The date each study was rejected","The name of the reviewer who disagreed","The reason it was excluded","The reviewer's personal opinion of it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because listing excluded studies together with the rationale lets a reader judge whether the criteria were applied fairly. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A study has a statistical power of 0.90. What is its probability of a type II error?","choices":["0.01","0.05","0.90","0.10"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: power is 1 minus the type II error probability, so the probability of a type II error is 1 minus 0.90, or 0.10. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What characterizes HIGH certainty regarding net benefit?","choices":["Consistent results from well-designed studies in representative populations","Evidence insufficient to assess effects on health outcomes","Evidence sufficient to determine effects, but constrained confidence","Evidence drawn entirely from expert consensus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence is stated directly: the conclusion is unlikely to be strongly affected by the results of future studies. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A town of 1,000 has 80 cases of a disease in 2015, and 20 previously healthy people develop it between 2016 and 2018. Which figure is the incidence for that period?","choices":["Eight percent","Ten percent","Eighteen percent","Two percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since 20 new cases in 1,000 people is the new-case count over the period. The 100 cases present in 2018 give a prevalence of ten percent. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why are predictive values influenced by prevalence?","choices":["Prevalence changes the sensitivity of the test","They include both diseased and healthy people","They are calculated within a single disease column","Prevalence alters the gold standard result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because each reads across a test-result row, which mixes the two disease columns in a proportion set by how common the disease is. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does longitudinal research measure?","choices":["A single moment in a population","The effect of an assigned intervention","Changes over time","Whether a program or policy is working"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is observational: the investigator records what changes rather than producing the change. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does a phase II trial aim to identify?","choices":["Definitive evidence of effect","Preliminary effect and dose relationship","A safe, tolerated dose","Uncommon side effects after release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: phase II also uses a small number of participants, and looks for a preliminary effect and any relationship of the effect to dose. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
